    {
      "key": "ocean_observation_data_sharing",
      "title": "[DEMO] How should countries and institutions share ocean-observation data while preserving trust and capacity?",
      "description": "FLAGSHIP SIMULATION — no initial position is implied.\n\nContext: IOC priorities emphasize ocean observations, interoperable data architectures, international cooperation and capacity development. Current Ocean Decade initiatives also focus on turning fragmented data into actionable coastal insights.\n\nTrigger: A fictional multinational observing network must adopt a data-sharing compact after partners report incompatible systems, unequal capacity and delays in access.\n\nDecision task: Negotiate data access levels, interoperability requirements, attribution, capacity support, quality controls and emergency-access provisions.\n\nCommon evidence baseline: IOC Executive Council adopts key decisions to strengthen ocean science, resilience and international cooperation; Ocean Decade endorsement accelerates partnerships and data sharing for coastal resilience; Looking back at 2025: a landmark year for ocean science in action; Recommendation on Open Science.\n\nStructured casting, evidence URLs, uncertainties, event injects, round plan and cross-World links are in the seed flagship source file.",
      "status": "open",
      "category": "ocean-data"
    },
