      "title": "[DEMO] How should coastal resilience plans allocate protection, adaptation and relocation measures?",
      "description": "FLAGSHIP SIMULATION — no initial position is implied.\n\nContext: UNESCO-IOC work on coastal hazards emphasizes end-to-end warning, community preparedness, data integration and locally actionable resilience. Ocean planning increasingly combines science, social data and inclusive governance.\n\nTrigger: A fictional coastal region has funding for only part of its proposed resilience programme and must prioritize measures before the next planning cycle.\n\nDecision task: Produce a phased resilience portfolio, decision criteria, community safeguards, early-warning priorities and triggers for future relocation decisions.\n\nCommon evidence baseline: Building capacities for the next generation of tsunami and coastal-hazard warning systems; Ocean Decade endorsement accelerates partnerships and data sharing for coastal resilience; IOC five-year Strategy for Ocean Planning and Management; IOC Executive Council adopts key decisions to strengthen ocean science, resilience and international cooperation.\n\nStructured casting, evidence URLs, uncertainties, event injects, round plan and cross-World links are in the seed flagship source file.",
